// A send cap of two segments: a write of 3000 takes two segments of 988, the peer's MSS of 1000 less the timestamp
// option, and write reports what was accepted; the rest waits for the ACK, which frees both entries, so the next
// write's 1024 bytes leave as a segment of 988 and one of 36. Linux has no segment cap: its write copies all 3000
// bytes into the socket's send buffer and sends what the windows allow.
--tcp_send_segments=2
0 socket(..., SOCK_STREAM, IPPROTO_TCP) = 3
+0 bind(3, ..., ...) = 0
+0 listen(3, 1) = 0

+0 < S 0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1000,nop,nop,TS val 100 ecr 0>
+0 > S. 0:0(0) ack 1 win 46720 <mss 1460,nop,nop,TS val 0 ecr 100>
+0 < . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 65535 <nop,nop,TS val 100 ecr 0>
+0 accept(3, ..., ...) = 4

+0 write(4, ..., 3000) = 1976
+0 > P. 1:989(988) ack 1 win 46720 <nop,nop,TS val 0 ecr 100>
+0 > P. 989:1977(988) ack 1 win 46720 <nop,nop,TS val 0 ecr 100>
+0 < . 1:1(0) ack 1977 win 65535 <nop,nop,TS val 100 ecr 0>
+0 write(4, ..., 1024) = 1024
+0 > P. 1977:2965(988) ack 1 win 46720 <nop,nop,TS val 0 ecr 100>
+0 > P. 2965:3001(36) ack 1 win 46720 <nop,nop,TS val 0 ecr 100>
+0 < . 1:1(0) ack 3001 win 65535 <nop,nop,TS val 100 ecr 0>
